---
import type { Project } from '../data/projects';
import { withBase } from '../utils/paths';

interface Props {
  project: Project;
}

const { project } = Astro.props;
---

<nav class="case-article__toc" aria-label="Case study sections">
  <span class="case-article__toc-label">On this page</span>
  <div class="case-article__toc-items">
    <a href="#fitness-overview">Project Overview</a>
    <a href="#fitness-data">Data &amp; Preparation</a>
    <a href="#fitness-questions">Business Questions</a>
    <a href="#fitness-pages">Dashboard Pages</a>
    <a href="#fitness-findings">What the Dashboard Reveals</a>
    <a href="#fitness-recommendations">Recommendations</a>
  </div>
</nav>

<section id="fitness-overview">
  <h2>1. Project Overview</h2>
  <p><strong>Fitness Center Membership, Revenue &amp; Retention Analytics</strong> is a multi-page Power BI challenge project representing MyGym Fitness Center. It explores how membership, subscriptions, revenue, retention indicators and gym usage can be understood together. This is an analytical challenge project, not a commercial deployment for a real fitness business.</p>
  <p>The aim was to bring practical business questions into one report: where revenue comes from, how membership and subscription groups compare, what the churn indicators show, how members use the gym and how results differ by location.</p>
  <div class="fitness-kpi-grid" aria-label="Executive Summary dashboard KPIs">
    <div class="fitness-kpi"><strong>$68.9K</strong><span>Total Revenue</span></div>
    <div class="fitness-kpi"><strong>$34.5</strong><span>Average Final Price</span></div>
    <div class="fitness-kpi"><strong>$33.8K</strong><span>Total Active Revenue</span></div>
    <div class="fitness-kpi"><strong>$35.1K</strong><span>Loss Revenue card; associated with Projected Lost Revenue</span></div>
  </div>
  <figure class="case-image case-image--gallery fitness-dashboard-image">
    <img
      class="case-image__dashboard"
      src={withBase(project.image ?? '/images/projects/fitness-centre-analytics.webp')}
      alt={project.imageAlt ?? ''}
      width={project.imageWidth ?? 1200}
      height={project.imageHeight ?? 707}
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <figcaption>{project.imageCaption}</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="fitness-data">
  <h2>2. Data Preparation</h2>
  <p>Before building the dashboard, I prepared the fitness centre dataset to make it suitable for analysis in Power BI. The preparation process involved working with the CSV dataset, organising the columns, assigning appropriate data types and preparing the member records for reporting.</p>

  <h3>1. Importing the data</h3>
  <p>I imported the prepared fitness dataset, <code>cleaned_fitness_data.csv</code>, into Power BI using Power Query. The dataset contained information about members, membership types, gym locations, visits, subscriptions and other fitness-related activities.</p>

  <h3>2. Organising the dataset</h3>
  <p>I promoted the first row to become the column headers, making the fields easier to identify and work with. I also assigned appropriate data types to the columns. For example, age and visit frequency were treated as numerical values, membership information as text, and attendance-related fields according to their data types.</p>
  <p>This helped ensure that the information could be used appropriately in calculations, comparisons and dashboard visuals.</p>

  <h3>3. Preparing membership records</h3>
  <p>I worked on the member identification field to provide each record with an identifier for reporting purposes. The Power Query process included creating a temporary identifier using the gym location, joining date and row index. This temporary column was subsequently removed, and the existing index column was renamed to <code>Members ID</code>.</p>

  <h3>4. Removing duplicate records</h3>
  <p>I applied Power Query's duplicate-removal operation to the prepared dataset. This helped prevent identical records from appearing more than once in the final dataset. The exact number of duplicate records removed was not documented.</p>

  <h3>5. Preparing the data for analysis</h3>
  <p>After preparation, the dataset was ready to support the dashboard analysis. The fields were organised to help explore membership performance, revenue, subscription patterns, member engagement, retention and differences across gym locations.</p>
  <p>The prepared data was then used to develop the Power BI report, with DAX measures and visualisations supporting the different areas of analysis.</p>

  <h3>Outcome</h3>
  <p>The preparation process provided a more organised dataset for analysis and reporting. It established a foundation for examining how membership activity, revenue and customer behaviour relate to the overall performance of the fitness centre.</p>
</section>

<section id="fitness-questions">
  <h2>3. Business Questions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>How is reported revenue distributed across membership types and member groups?</li>
    <li>How do subscription models, prices and discounts compare?</li>
    <li>What churn and churn-risk indicators does the report display?</li>
    <li>How do visit patterns, time in the gym and training activity vary?</li>
    <li>How do revenue, subscriptions and usage differ across gym locations?</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="fitness-pages">
  <h2>4. Dashboard Pages</h2>
  <div class="fitness-page">
    <h3>Executive Summary</h3>
    <p>Brings headline revenue and pricing indicators together with monthly revenue from new subscribers and breakdowns by age, membership type and gender. It gives users an overview and helps identify which member groups to explore further.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fitness-page">
    <h3>Revenue</h3>
    <p>Includes tenure, revenue-per-day, discount and drink-subscription indicators, alongside revenue comparisons by location, membership type, age, gender and discount type. These views can help teams identify patterns to investigate before adjusting offers or prioritising segments.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fitness-page">
    <h3>Subscription</h3>
    <p>Compares subscription price, revenue, discounts and displayed churn measures across subscription models and locations. It provides context for reviewing plan differences and retention, with measure definitions to be confirmed before decisions are made.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fitness-page">
    <h3>Membership Analysis</h3>
    <p>Looks at member counts, monthly new-member acquisition, membership mix, churn by age and membership type, tenure and selected engagement indicators such as personal-training hours. This helps users understand how the member base is changing and identify where further retention analysis may be useful.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fitness-page">
    <h3>Engagement &amp; Usage</h3>
    <p>Brings together visits per week, gym duration and peak usage indicators, check-in and check-out times, and personal-training and group-lesson activity. It helps a fitness business explore how members use its services and where engagement or resource-planning questions merit further study.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fitness-page">
    <h3>Geographic Analysis</h3>
    <p>Uses location comparisons and maps for revenue, projected loss and churn, alongside subscription, age, sauna and drink-subscription views. It supports exploration of differences between gyms; no location ranking is claimed here.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fitness-page">
    <h3>General Analysis</h3>
    <p>Combines comparisons of drink subscriptions, sauna use, discounts and personal-training adoption and performance, with filters for member and location attributes. It supports flexible exploration of questions across the business.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="fitness-findings">
  <h2>5. What the Dashboard Reveals</h2>
  <p>The Executive Summary screenshot displays <strong>$68.9K Total Revenue</strong>, <strong>$34.5 Average Final Price</strong>, <strong>$33.8K Total Active Revenue</strong> and <strong>$35.1K on the Loss Revenue card</strong>.</p>
  <p>The supplied dashboard description also reports an <strong>Average Subscription of 40.1</strong>, a <strong>Churn Rate of 50.6%</strong> and a <strong>Low Risk</strong> churn-risk score. The churn and risk definitions are unavailable, so these are reported dashboard outputs rather than a validated conclusion about member risk.</p>
  <p>Across the report, visuals compare revenue and membership measures by subscription model, age group, membership type, gender, discount and gym location. The available evidence confirms these comparisons, but it does not support a reliable ranking of top-performing segments or quantified findings about usage and location.</p>
</section>

<section id="fitness-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Practical Recommendations</h2>
  <p>These recommendations are informed by the analysis areas in the dashboard; they are not proven business outcomes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Revenue improvement:</strong> Review revenue alongside member volumes and membership mix to identify segments or offers for closer investigation.</li>
    <li><strong>Membership retention:</strong> Validate the churn-rate definition and “Low Risk” thresholds, then use clearly defined member-level measures to guide retention discussions.</li>
    <li><strong>Subscription pricing:</strong> Compare subscription prices and discounts with membership uptake and revenue before testing plan or offer changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Member engagement:</strong> Examine visits, gym time and training indicators by membership type to identify services that may merit further engagement initiatives.</li>
    <li><strong>Gym location performance:</strong> Compare locations using consistent measures and time periods before deciding where to direct operational attention.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
